A||||10||saleenfiero||The Great Audio Maker Ripoff Scam||||1||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||06-26-2001||04:43 PM||||Okay, maybe it is just me again, but I am totally surprised at what I heard when I went looking for a new headunit for my car.  I have a 2 yr old Kenwood Excelon deck in my car right now.  It's hooked up to a CD changer.  Fine, I figure some of these new head units are pretty spiffy(?) and I want one.  I find that new sony one is pretty cool, don't know model #, but its the one with no buttons and ONE remote, costs about $470 US.  THEN I find that my CD changer won't match anything but another Kenwood deck!  What kind of crap is this?  Is there any adapaters out there to mate these two models together?  I don't care about small incompatibilities like losing the name CD feature.....<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||06-26-2001||05:47 PM||||i've heard off and on that certain companies and models will control/be controled by other brands, but it seems the people who know aren't talking. <P>i've got an alpine radio that is a hidaway box with a small control panel and a pioneer changer that is din size. they would be great together if they were compatible. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||06-26-2001||05:54 PM||||I don't really know of any two different company's components that are interchangeable like that. I don't think there is really any way to do that, except using FM modulation. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DecadenceR||06-26-2001||06:16 PM||||there are a very very very few amount of specialty stereo shops that do have connectors for some types. I know the one near me, Tronix, had one that I needed a few years back. I think it cost me $25. I'd just call a shop hear you (don't go in, they'll try to get you to buy stuff) and see what direction they point you in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||grinthock||06-26-2001||06:29 PM||||90% of the time your changer, kenwood must go with a kenwood deck, sony changer == sony deck, (Or Sony FM Modulator kit), clarion with a clarion deck.... There are some adaptors made by PIE and other companies that allow different changers on decks but it's usually like Kenwood changer on a A/C Delco deck (use your kenwood on your STOCK deck that has changer controls such as a 98 S-10 deck kinda deal) but as for using your kenwood changer on a sony deck forget it...<P>I agree it's stupid someone should come up with a standard and everyone should STICK TO IT!  Something like the IEEE-1394 "FireWire" (Name copyrighted by apple computer) or USB2 or even the regular USB would be fine!  My personal preference would be USB for the controls then send the audio out as LINE OUT so we could use the changers with lots of stuff, computers, decks, stereo's and custom stuff!<P>My thoughts........<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://grinthock.2y.net/pics/87gt/S_P000386.JPG">   <P>Home page: <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroworld.com</A> Home  of the fiero voting booth!<BR>Owner of 85 GT<BR>87 GT Silver<BR>1995 Ford Escort Wagoon POS||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||06-26-2001||06:58 PM||||Not only do you have non-interchangeability (phew!) between brands, but you also have non-interchangeability within brands, For example, Alpines AI-net and M-bus, one doesn't work with the other. That's just queer if you ask me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-26-2001||07:51 PM||||grinthock--RF modulated CD changers will work with any radio/stereo that can pick up an FM signal.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000007||saleenfiero||06-26-2001||10:32 PM||||Personally I got something against FM mod decks.  And I have two perfectly good kenwood changers.  Oh well, I guess I wait until kenwood put out a half assed deck, maybe if I ever get a CD burner, I'll go against my morals and get an MP3 compatible one.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wannabe||06-26-2001||11:27 PM||||Yes, shaftola. Worse yet, the adapters are different by auto make too. Clarion makes some of the high end OEM systems, particularly for some European models. You thiknk you could get a Clarion CD changer to plug into the factory head unit? No way! It has to be OEM for some fifty zillion bucks. I think the price was $860 for a cd changer. Yikes!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tom88gt||06-27-2001||07:00 PM||||It's all greed...stereo companies want you to buy only their stuff.  No way are they gonna make it easy for you to buy someone else's CD changer to use with their head unit.  They are almost as bad as the car makers.||||reg||8
Z||000010||DJRice||06-27-2001||08:27 PM||||That has pretty well been standard practice among those companies who are into Car Audio.  Ive never heard of interchangability....and even if someone could build adaptors to mate different changers to different decks, there would have to be hundreds of adaptors out there...and you would never get all of the features to match.  My Sony Custom File Changer might play through a Kenwood deck with some adaptation, but I would never be able to get the disc naming or programming capabilities out of it.  <P>Id even be surprised if your old Kenwood Changer worked with a new Kenwood head unit.<P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
